## 2 Scale-free analysis

A primary feature of network analyses is the study of the network’s degree distribution, and one of the most prevalent beliefs is the ubiquity of scale-free networks ([Albert and Barabási, 2002](https://arxiv.org/html/2208.01208v2#bib.bib1)). However, a more recent survey concludes that scale-free networks are rare ([Broido and Clauset, 2019](https://arxiv.org/html/2208.01208v2#bib.bib5)). Using the same methodology, we classify the the email networks at the team level, which is summarized in Figure [S1](https://arxiv.org/html/2208.01208v2#S2.F1a "Fig S1 ‣ 2 Scale-free analysis ‣ Supplementary Material:Communication network dynamics in a large organizational hierarchy"). Note that in our analysis, we include every team even though only 9 of the 88 teams satisfies the original inclusion criteria from [Broido and Clauset (2019)](https://arxiv.org/html/2208.01208v2#bib.bib5) that the mean degree is less than n\sqrt{n}.

